# Types beyond Hindley-Milner, written with the choice and the parallel:
# a choice of types is a union type, and applying a function to the choice
# of its inputs gives the choice of its outputs; a parallel of types is an
# intersection type. Equations of this kind exist, and a derivation of each needs lemma instances at classes that
# are no position of the equation, which the greedy descent never visits.
#
# Every lemma here is a theorem of λ-lattice-unit-η: the two distribution
# laws are the binary instances of equations (8) and (9) on the IR's choice
# and parallel layers (CONTRIBUTING.md, "Terms"), and the other three are
# beta laws of defines. Each lemma's declaration cites its proof on paper.
# A choice of more than two members needs a law of its own arity; none is
# declared, since no example needs one.

# Equation (8), a choice of two members in function position: (M + N) P = M P + N P.
λ export
. (λtuple. tuple λmixin. mixin mixin)
λ omega
. omega (omega (omega (omega corpora "library") "prelude") "pair")
λ pair _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega corpora "library") "lattice_types") "self_apply")
λ self_apply
. omega (omega (omega (omega corpora "library") "prelude") "combinators")
λ identity _ _ _ _ _ _
. export
λ _ rewriting _ _
. rewriting
λ _ lemmas _
. lemmas
  # The beta law of identity, the statement of prelude's identity_applied
  # (one address, two names), declared here as well so that the examples can
  # enable this module without prelude's lemmas: matched outside the
  # positions, natural_equal_zero_zero from right to left plants
  # natural_equal zero zero in the class of true, and
  # natural_equal_successor_successor from right to left then adds
  # natural_equal (successor m) (successor n) for every natural_equal m n
  # there, a new binding each time: with prelude's lemmas enabled, example C
  # spends its budget of 64 there under both strategies, 1 instance of the
  # one and 63 of the other (the section's readings). The e-graph interns an
  # application whose argument an ignored hole drops (true x y, false x y,
  # konstant x y) as identity applied to what remains, so this law is also
  # their beta law.
  # FIXME (2026-10-04): this lemma duplicates prelude's identity_applied, a https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/2010
  # workaround for the granularity of the option lemma_modules, which enables
  # whole modules. Candidate fixes: move the beta laws of the generic
  # combinators (identity_applied, apply_to_applied, compose_applied) out of
  # prelude into a module of their own that the examples enable, or keep
  # prelude's natural_equal lemmas out of a run by a finer option.
  ( λ s
    . s (λk. k "identity_returns_its_argument") (λx_0. identity x_0) (λx_0. x_0) λ_ md _. md λk. k "[identity_returns_its_argument](docs/design/gezi-normality-measure.md)"
  # The beta law of self_apply: self_apply x is x x.
  + λ s
    . s (λk. k "self_apply_applied") (λx_0. self_apply x_0) (λx_0. x_0 x_0) λ_ md _. md λk. k "[self_apply_applied](docs/design/gezi-normality-measure.md)"
  # The beta law of pair: pair l r p is p l r.
  + λ s
    . s (λk. k "pair_applied") (λx_0 x_1 x_2. pair x_0 x_1 x_2) (λx_0 x_1 x_2. x_2 x_0 x_1) λ_ md _. md λk. k "[pair_applied](docs/design/gezi-normality-measure.md)"
  # Equation (9), a parallel in function position: (M ∥ N) P = M P ∥ N P.
  + λ s
    . s (λk. k "parallel_applied") (λx_0 x_1 x_2. (x_0 ∥ x_1) x_2) (λx_0 x_1 x_2. (x_0 x_2 ∥ x_1 x_2)) λ_ md _. md λk. k "[parallel_applied](docs/design/gezi-normality-measure.md)"
  + λ s
    . s (λk. k "choice_applied") (λx_0 x_1 x_2. (x_1 + x_0) x_2) (λx_0 x_1 x_2. (x_1 x_2 + x_0 x_2)) λ_ md _. md λk. k "[choice_applied](docs/design/gezi-normality-measure.md)"
  )
